// Unit 2 Lesson 5 â€” 30-question bank

::U2L5Q01::FITT stands for {=Frequency, Intensity, Time, and Type~Fitness, Interval, Training, and Testing~Force, Intensity, Tempo, and Technique~Frequency, Improvement, Time, and Training}

::U2L5Q02::Frequency asks {=How often?~How hard?~How long?~What activity?}

::U2L5Q03::Intensity asks {=How hard?~How often?~How long?~What activity?}

::U2L5Q04::Time asks {=How long?~How hard?~How often?~What equipment?}

::U2L5Q05::Type asks {=What activity?~How often?~How long?~How much recovery?}

::U2L5Q06::Overload means {=an appropriate challenge beyond the current usual demand~maximum effort every session~pain during training~changing every variable at once}

::U2L5Q07::Progression means {=gradual purposeful adjustment over time~increasing every workout~removing recovery~using one exercise only}

::U2L5Q08::Specificity means {=matching training demands to the fitness goal~performing only one exercise forever~training at maximum intensity~using the same plan for everyone}

::U2L5Q09::Recovery is important because it {=supports response, adaptation, and continued participation~slows all improvement~replaces specificity~requires no monitoring}

::U2L5Q10::Individualization means {=fitting decisions to the person and circumstances~giving everyone the same plan~requiring private medical disclosure~ignoring access barriers}

::U2L5Q11::Which is a FITT variable rather than a training principle? {=Time~Progression~Specificity~Recovery}

::U2L5Q12::Increasing a walk from 15 to 20 minutes changes {=Time~Type~Frequency~Specificity}

::U2L5Q13::That 15-to-20-minute change may apply {=progression and overload~only specificity~only individualization~no training principle}

::U2L5Q14::A cardio goal is best matched with {=an activity that meaningfully challenges the cardiorespiratory system~an unrelated activity~complete rest only~maximum resistance only}

::U2L5Q15::A student increases frequency, intensity, time, and complexity at once. Best critique? {=The changes are excessive and make response difficult to monitor.~It guarantees adaptation.~It is always required for overload.~Recovery is unnecessary.}

::U2L5Q16::A safer revision usually begins by {=changing one or two variables purposefully and monitoring response~doubling all demands~removing rest days~ignoring baseline evidence}

::U2L5Q17::Which evidence best supports progression? {=The current manageable plan is becoming easier and recovery is adequate.~The student wants immediate results.~A friend uses a harder plan.~Pain occurs every session.}

::U2L5Q18::A weather barrier is best addressed through {=an accessible indoor option that preserves the goal and appropriate intensity~canceling recovery~adding maximum intensity~changing to an unrelated goal}

::U2L5Q19::For muscular endurance, an appropriate change may be {=a modest increase in controlled repetitions or duration~maximal repetitions with lost form~no recovery~an unrelated aerobic-only plan}

::U2L5Q20::Which statement about overload is correct? {=It can come from a modest change in frequency, intensity, time, resistance, or movement demand.~It always means maximal effort.~It requires pain.~It must change every FITT variable.}

::U2L5Q21::Which statement about progression is correct? {=It is based on response and need, not an automatic increase every workout.~It means increasing daily.~It excludes recovery.~It guarantees a fixed adaptation rate.}

::U2L5Q22::Which statement about specificity is correct? {=Several activities can support the same goal if their demands are relevant.~Only one exercise can ever support a goal.~Specificity is a FITT variable.~Specificity means maximum intensity.}

::U2L5Q23::Training challenge followed by appropriate recovery may lead to {=adaptation that should be monitored before adjustment~guaranteed improvement at a fixed rate~no need for individualization~automatic maximal performance}

::U2L5Q24::A plateau should lead first to {=reviewing evidence, recovery, goal alignment, and possible modest adjustment~changing everything immediately~eliminating recovery~assuming failure}

::U2L5Q25::Which plan is most sustainable? {=A realistic, accessible, goal-aligned plan with gradual changes and recovery~a plan requiring unavailable equipment~daily maximal work~a plan unrelated to preferences or schedule}

::U2L5Q26::RPE is most directly used to monitor {=Intensity~Frequency~Type~Duration}

::U2L5Q27::Three sessions per week describes {=Frequency~Intensity~Time~Type}

::U2L5Q28::Stationary cycling describes {=Type~Frequency~Intensity~Progression}

::U2L5Q29::A 25-minute session describes {=Time~Specificity~Recovery~Frequency}

::U2L5Q30::The strongest plan justification {=cites baseline evidence, names the changed variable and principle, and explains safety and goal alignment~says harder is always better~copies another personâ€™s plan~lists activities without reasons}


